course: politics
lesson: 40
title: Putting it together: explain each side, check the claim
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This last lesson puts the whole course together in three habits: know who decides, check the claim, and explain each side in its best words before you say what you think.
explain: Who decides matters: the national government runs things like the army, while local councils or school boards usually decide things like schools, bins and parks, so a new playground is not the Prime Minister's job.
explain: Checking is the habit from the information-wars lessons: who says so, how do they know, who else reports it, find the full clip, and "not sure yet" is allowed.
explain: Explaining each side is like a referee who knows both teams' tactics without wearing either shirt: a sugar tax, an age limit for social media, how to end a war, each has people who think hard and disagree.
explain: Some things are facts as widely reported, like who invaded whom, and some are arguments people still have; a good citizen keeps the two apart, argues kindly, and tells people honestly what happened, like a class rep at the end of the term.
fact: National governments decide big national things like the army; local councils or school boards usually decide local things like schools, bins and parks.
fact: To verify a claim, look on official websites and big news sites; if nothing official has it, it is probably not true, and you can say so without laughing at anyone.
fact: It was reported that some governments taxed sugary drinks to help people drink less sugar; some say it helps health, others say it costs poorer families more and people should choose for themselves.
fact: As widely reported, Russia began a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022, and most countries at the UN voted to call on Russia to withdraw its troops.
fact: On how to end the war, some say Ukraine needs weapons to defend itself, others worry that makes the war last longer and want more talks.
fact: Cartoons and comedy exaggerate politicians on purpose to make people laugh and think; real politicians differ, and some are good at their jobs while others are not.
fact: Steelmanning each other's best reason before arguing helps two people understand each other and find a middle way.
fact: A five-second clip can cut out a single word like "not" and turn the meaning upside down, so find the full speech.
fact: It was reported that some countries planned or made rules keeping children under 16 off social media; some say it protects children, others say it is hard to enforce and kids should learn to use apps safely.
fact: As widely reported, Hamas attacked Israel on 7 October 2023, killing many people and taking hostages, and then Israel fought a war in Gaza where many civilians were killed and many went hungry; both peoples have suffered.
fact: On that war, some say Israel had to fight to bring hostages home and stop future attacks; others say the war hurt too many civilians and must stop, with more help for Gaza.
fact: Honest leaders explain what happened even when they lose; some say the best leaders get results, others say the best are fair and honest even if they win less.
fact: Words in this lesson: "skibidi" is a word kids shout from online videos without any real meaning; "ate" means did it brilliantly; "pop" and "soda" are two US words for a fizzy drink; "do the needful" in Indian English means do what is needed.
exercise: Who usually decides whether a school gets a new playground? | The local council or school board | The Prime Minister alone, from an office in London | The army | The United Nations
exercise: What does the national government usually decide? | Big national things like the army | Which bins your street uses | The colour of your school's doors | What your class has for lunch
exercise: A post says kids must wear helmets to walk to school, and nothing official has it. What should Mia say? | "I can't find this anywhere official, so it's probably fake." | "It's true, everyone's sharing it." | "It's fake and you're all silly for believing it." | "Let's share it to warn more people."
exercise: Ethan's dad groans about a fizzy-drink tax and his mum says "Good idea". What can Ethan do? | Explain both sides without saying who is right | Decide his dad is right because he is louder | Decide his mum is right because she spoke last | Say taxes have nothing to do with health
exercise: What reason do supporters give for a sugar tax? | People buy fewer sugary drinks, which helps health | It makes fizzy drinks much cheaper for everyone in the country | It stops every shop from selling bottled water to children | It pays for more sweets and fizzy drinks to be given out in schools
exercise: What reason do opponents give against a sugar tax? | It costs poorer families more and people should choose | It makes drinks far too cheap | It forces everyone to drink more sugar than they did before the tax | It is a tax on vegetables
exercise: A classmate says Ukraine started the war with Russia. What has been widely reported? | Russia began a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022 | Ukraine invaded Russia in 2022 | There has never been any war there | The war was started by the United Nations
exercise: What did most countries at the UN vote for? | To call on Russia to withdraw its troops | To call on Ukraine to give up its land to Russia | To leave the UN for good and close it down | To ban all news about the war around the world
exercise: A cartoon shows the president as a silly duck. What is the cartoon doing? | Exaggerating on purpose to make people laugh and think | Showing exactly what all politicians are really like at home | Reporting the news as plain fact | Proving politicians are really ducks
exercise: Grace and Liam disagree about a longer lunch. What deal helps them? | Each says the other's best reason before arguing | Each tries to shout the other down until one of them gives up | They let the loudest kid decide | They stop being friends
exercise: A five-second clip shows a politician saying "We will close all schools". What did the full speech show? | She said "We will not close all schools" | She said exactly the same thing in the full speech | She never gave a speech at all; the clip was made up | She said she would close all the parks, not the schools
exercise: What do the two sides say about keeping under-16s off social media? | It protects kids, or it is hard to enforce and kids should learn safely | Everyone agrees it is a perfect idea | Nobody has ever talked about it | It only affects grown-ups
exercise: Fatima's friend says the Israel-Gaza war is "simple: one side is good, one is bad". What does Fatima remember? | Both peoples have suffered, and people disagree about many parts | Only one side has suffered at all, so the other side doesn't matter | Nothing has been reported about it | The war never really happened
exercise: Which is a widely reported fact about 7 October 2023? | Hamas attacked Israel, killing many people and taking hostages | Israel and Hamas signed a peace treaty that day | Nothing happened that day | The United Nations attacked both sides
exercise: Noah says, "I didn't win everything we asked for, but I always told you what happened." What does that build? | Trust | Fear | Clickbait | A filter bubble
exercise: What are the three habits this course ends with? | Know who decides, check the claim, explain each side | Pick a side, shout, share fast | Trust the likes, trust the logos, trust every screenshot you see | Ignore news, ignore votes, ignore others
test: "skibidi, the PM's gonna build our new playground!" says a little brother. What is the better version? | Local councils or school boards usually decide that | Yes, the Prime Minister picks every playground in the country | The army builds all playgrounds | Nobody decides, playgrounds just appear
test: "helmets to WALK to school?? new law, it's all over the group chat, no cap" — nothing on the government site or big news. What is best? | Say it is probably fake and show where you checked | Buy a helmet tonight, just in case the law turns out to be real | Laugh at everyone in the group chat who believed it was true | Share it with more group chats so that everyone else gets warned
test: In Ohio: "Tax on pop? That's dumb," says Dad. "It's smart," says Mom. Which reply ate? | Explaining each side's best reason without taking one | Agreeing with whoever is louder, because they must feel it more | Saying both of them are dumb | Saying the tax is about popcorn
test: "Ukraine started it, my cousin said, end of" — what is the most careful reply? | It was widely reported that Russia invaded Ukraine in 2022; let's check together | You are right, your cousin always knows | Nobody knows anything about any war | Wars are never reported in the news
test: "politicians are all ducks, I saw it in a cartoon lol" — what is true? | Cartoons exaggerate on purpose, and real politicians differ | All politicians are exactly like the cartoon, it's drawn from real life | Cartoons are a kind of official news | Politicians write all the cartoons about them
test: What would happen if Grace and Liam each had to say the other's best reason first? | They would understand each other and could find a middle way | They would both forget what they wanted in the first place and go home | One of them would still have to win everything and the other get nothing | They would never be allowed to argue about anything at school again
test: "she said she'll close ALL schools, watch, 5 seconds, proof" — what is the first thing to check? | The full speech, in case words like "not" were cut | How many people shared the clip | Whether the clip has scary music | Whether your friends are angry about it too, because that proves it
test: An older cousin says, "Under-16 social media ban? Total rubbish." The teacher says, "It might help." How would a careful pupil describe it? | Some say it protects kids, others say it is hard to enforce | Only the cousin can be right, because he is older and uses social media | Only the teacher can be right | Nobody disagrees about this at all
test: "Israel-Gaza is simple, one side's goodies, one side's baddies" — what fits the lesson best? | Both peoples have suffered and people disagree about many parts | It is simple and everyone in the world agrees on who the goodies are | Nothing at all has been reported about it | It is a cartoon, not a real war
test: In Delhi: "Class rep, kindly do the needful and tell us what happened at council." What makes a good report back? | Honestly saying what was asked, what was won and what was not | Saying everything was won, even if it was not, so the class stays happy | Keeping the meeting a secret | Blaming the other reps for everything
test: Two friends: one says "the best leader gets results", the other "the best leader is honest and fair". What is true? | People disagree, and each view has a reason | Only the first friend can be right, because results are all that count | Only the second friend can be right, because honesty is all that counts | Leaders do not need either quality to do the job well
test: Your little sister asks, "What's the one thing to do before sharing anything about politics?" Which answer sums up the course? | Check who says it, how they know and who else reports it | Count the likes and share if there are lots | Share fast before anyone else does | Only share it if it makes you angry
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the authority ordinarily responsible for the provision of a school playground? | The local council or school board | The Prime Minister, acting in person | The armed forces of the state | The United Nations
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the events of February 2022, as widely reported. | The Russian Federation commenced a full-scale invasion of Ukraine | Ukraine commenced a full-scale invasion of the Russian Federation | No hostilities whatsoever were reported | The United Nations commenced hostilities against both states
exam: It is given that a recording of five seconds' duration appears to report an intention to close all schools. Identify the course of action most appropriate. | Consultation of the complete recording, lest a word altering the meaning have been omitted | Immediate circulation of the recording to all acquaintances, lest they remain uninformed of the danger | Acceptance of the recording upon the strength of its brevity | Reliance upon the number of persons who have viewed it
exam: Whereas a pupil is asked to account for a dispute concerning a proposed tax upon sugary drinks, determine the response most consistent with the aims of this course. | A fair statement of the principal reason advanced by each side, without taking a side | A declaration in favour of whichever party speaks the loudest | A refusal to discuss the matter whatsoever | An assertion that such disputes have no bearing upon health
